WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.102
Milvus and Zilliz are commonly discussed for larger vector-search workloads.

00:00:05.102 --> 00:00:07.342
The pattern is still the same.

00:00:07.342 --> 00:00:15.332
Create or connect to a collection, define the vector dimension and metric, insert vectors with associated fields,

00:00:15.332 --> 00:00:18.262
and search using the query vector.

00:00:18.262 --> 00:00:24.277
The classroom example focuses on the search pattern rather than a full production deployment.

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:34.009
At larger scale, additional concerns appear: index type, partitioning, replication, memory usage, deployment topology,

00:00:34.009 --> 00:00:42.819
and operational monitoring. Those are important topics, but they come after the basic RAG architecture is clear.

00:00:42.819 --> 00:00:52.534
I want you to see that even when the infrastructure becomes more sophisticated, the application still needs good chunks,

00:00:52.534 --> 00:00:58.794
appropriate embeddings, useful metadata, and a way to evaluate retrieval quality.

00:00:58.794 --> 00:01:01.634
Scale does not replace the fundamentals.
